# Contributor Guide

Thank you for your interest in contributing to Nexy. Please review the [Code of Conduct](https://github.com/NexyPy/nexy/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md) first.

## Quick start

```bash
# Fork and clone
git clone https://github.com/YOUR_USERNAME/nexy.git
cd nexy

# Set up Python
uv venv
# Windows: .venv\Scripts\activate
# Unix/macOS: source .venv/bin/activate
uv pip install -e ".[dev]"

# Verify everything works
ruff check nexy/
ruff format nexy/ --check
python -m mypy nexy --strict
python -m pytest tests/ -v
```

Total time: ~2 minutes.

## Prerequisites

- **Python** 3.12+
- **Node.js** 20+ (for frontend builds and VS Code extension)
- **uv** package manager (recommended) or pip
- **pnpm** (for JavaScript dependencies — only needed for VS Code extension)

## Project structure

```
nexy/
├── nexy/                   # Main Python package
│   ├── cli/                # CLI commands (dev, build, start, new)
│   ├── compiler/           # .nexy → Jinja2 pipeline
│   │   ├── parser/         # Scanner, sanitizer, template, validator, logic
│   │   └── generator/      # Code generation
│   ├── routers/            # AppServer, FBRouter, ModularRouter
│   │   ├── fbrouter/       # File-based routing
│   │   └── actions/        # Server actions
│   ├── frontend/           # React, Vue, Svelte, Solid, Preact integrations
│   ├── vfs/                # Virtual File System
│   ├── core/               # Config, models, types
│   └── templates/          # Project scaffold templates
├── tests/                  # pytest suite
│   ├── unit/               # Unit tests mirroring nexy/ structure
│   └── integration/        # Integration tests
├── extensions/vscode/      # VS Code extension (LSP)
└── docs/                   # Documentation site (separate app)
```

## Development workflow

### 1. Pick an issue

Start with a [good first issue](https://github.com/NexyPy/nexy/issues?q=is%3Aissue+is%3Aopen+label%3A%22good+first+issue%22) or discuss your idea in [GitHub Discussions](https://github.com/NexyPy/nexy/discussions) before opening a PR for a new feature.

### 2. Create a branch

| Branch pattern | Purpose |
|---------------|---------|
| `feature/*` | New features |
| `fix/*` | Bug fixes |
| `docs/*` | Documentation |
| `refactor/*` | Code refactoring |
| `perf/*` | Performance |
| `test/*` | Tests |

```bash
git checkout -b feature/my-feature
```

### 3. Make changes

Follow the coding standards and write tests first (TDD).

### 4. Commit

Use [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/):

```
<type>(<scope>): <description>
```

Types: `feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `refactor`, `perf`, `test`, `chore`

```
feat(router): add wildcard route support
fix(compiler): handle empty frontmatter
docs: update README with CLI examples
```

### 5. Push and open a PR

```bash
git push origin feature/my-feature
```

Use the [PR template](https://github.com/NexyPy/nexy/blob/main/.github/PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE.md).

## Coding standards

### Python

- **Formatter**: Ruff (line-length=100, target-version=py312)
- **Linter**: Ruff (all rules)
- **Type checker**: mypy `--strict`

```bash
# Quality gates (run in this order)
ruff check nexy/
ruff format nexy/ --check
python -m mypy nexy --strict
```

### TypeScript (VS Code extension)

- **Linter**: ESLint (via `tsc -b`)
- **Format**: Prettier (via ESLint)

### General rules

- All code in English — no French comments or variable names
- Functions under 20 lines, classes under 10 methods
- No premature abstraction — KISS before clever
- Follow SOLID principles — one responsibility per file

## Testing

```bash
# All tests
python -m pytest tests/ -v

# Single test file
python -m pytest tests/unit/nexy/parser/test_scanner.py -v

# Coverage
python -m pytest tests/ --cov=nexy --cov-report=term
```

**Coverage target**: 95% per module.

### Conventions

- **TDD**: test first (red → green → refactor)
- One assertion per test
- Naming: `test_<thing>_<scenario>`
- Unit tests in `tests/unit/` mirroring the module path

## Pull request checklist

- [ ] `python -m pytest tests/ -v` — tests pass
- [ ] `ruff check nexy/` — lint passes
- [ ] `ruff format nexy/ --check` — formatting correct
- [ ] `python -m mypy nexy --strict` — type checks
- [ ] New tests cover my changes
- [ ] Documentation updated (if public API change)
- [ ] Conventional commit messages
- [ ] One module/feature per PR

### Review process

1. At least one maintainer review required
2. Address feedback with additional commits (no force-push during review)
3. Maintainers squash-merge approved PRs

## Release process (maintainers only)

```bash
# 1. Bump version in nexy/__version__.py + pyproject.toml

# 2. Update changelog
npx git-cliff -o CHANGELOG.md

# 3. Tag and push
git tag v<version>
git push origin v<version>

# 4. Publish to PyPI
uv build && uv publish

# 5. Create GitHub Release
```

## Getting help

- **Bug reports**: open a [GitHub Issue](https://github.com/NexyPy/nexy/issues/new/choose)
- **Questions**: [GitHub Discussions](https://github.com/NexyPy/nexy/discussions)
- **Security issues**: see [SECURITY.md](https://github.com/NexyPy/nexy/blob/main/SECURITY.md)
